ect_test.cljc` gains `mirror-budget-and-chase-budget-reach-the-session-mirror` and `dial-bounds-reach-projection-and-mirror`. `invalid-bounds-are-a-composition-error` gains mirror/chase rows, and its budget rows now also check `dial`.\n\n## Not in this slice\n\n- The spec's §1.2 also grows dial's `policy` `select-keys` by four S2b/S2c link keys and adds a 2-arity `dial-step! dial now`. Both depend on S2b/S2c link policy and were not in the S2a brief, so I left them out.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `clojure -M:test -n dao.stream.remote-test -n dao.stream.ws-project-test`: Ran 63 tests containing 479 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.\n- `clj -M:kondo --lint <4 files>`: 0 errors, 1 warning: `remote_test.cljc:661:13 unused binding peer2`. That warning was already there; this slice only appends to that file.\n- Not run: `bb test:clj` and the full 3-host `bb test` (Node, Dart). Run both before committing.\n# S2a reconcile report (claude-opus-5-5)\n\nWorktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2`, branch `stream-crossmachine-s2`, uncommitted.\n\n## R1: dial mirror effects inside an atom retry (fixed)\n\n- `src/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc`, `dial-step!`: `remote/mirror-step` no longer runs inside `swap!`. It now runs once over `@mirror-cursor`, and `reset!` stores the cursor it returns. This matches `accept-step!`'s single-driver discipline, and a comment says so.\n- Regression test: `ws_project_test.cljc` `dial-mirror-effects-run-once-per-step`.\n  - The served table entry is a writer that, during its first `append!`, replaces the dial's mirror cursor with an equal but distinct map. On the JVM that forces a CAS retry if the mirror runs inside `swap!`.\n  - It asserts that the source append ran exactly once and was answered once.\n  - Checked against the old `swap!` form: 2 failures (`[:v :v]` applied, `[1 1]` answered). With the fix it passes.\n  - The interference is one-shot. Interfering on every append livelocked the old form, and the run died with no report.\n  - On CLJS and CLJD `swap!` never retries, so there the test only confirms the normal path.\n\n## Coverage: writer `full` on `cursor` and `next` (added)\n\n- `remote_test.cljc` `a-full-writer-rewinds-idempotent-answers-and-drops-append-answers` gains a `cursor and next answers refused full rewind too` block. It runs once with a `cursor` request and once with a `next` request.\n- Each case puts a descriptor request ahead of the cursor/next request. The writer carries the first answer and refuses the second once with `full`.\n- It asserts that the step returns the cursor preceding the refused request, not the input cursor, and that the next call answers the same id.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `clojure -M:test -n dao.stream.remote-test -n dao.stream.ws-project-test`: Ran 64 tests containing 487 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.\n- `mise exec -- cljstyle check` on the 4 files: clean, no output.\n- `clj -M:kondo --lint` on the 4 files: errors 0, 1 warning (`remote_test.cljc:661:13 unused binding peer2`). That warning was already there and is not in code this slice touched.\n- Not run: `bb test:clj` and the full 3-host `bb test`.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-07 06:39:20 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-07 13:39:20 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: unavailable\n\n# Independent S2a review\n\n**Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED**\n\nReviewed the uncommitted five-file diff in `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2`, branch `stream-crossmachine-s2`, HEAD `65f635d0`, against the S2 architect specification §1.1, §1.2, §2 and §3's S2a plan, and mob consensus D1–D3/D5. No code, design, or test files were modified. Only this requested review artifact was written; no other worktree was accessed.\n\n## Blocking finding\n\n### R1 — P1: dial mirror effects execute inside an atom retry function\n\n**Location:** `src/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:587-590`; effect paths `src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:235` and `:161-168`.\n\n`dial-step!` calls `remote/mirror-step` inside the function supplied to `swap! mirror-cursor`. That function reads streams, invokes source operations (including append!), and writes answers. If the cursor atom changes before the compare-and-set commits, swap! executes those effects again. The cursor transaction cannot roll back an already accepted source append or answer write.\n\nThis fails the task's explicit checkpoint “Are all side-effects kept outside atom retry?” It also means the non-rewind branch for append! does not by itself prevent duplicate execution through the dial composition. A retry can exceed a tick's mirror budget.\n\n**Provenance:** This effectful swap! already existed at HEAD. S2a changes its body to pass bounds but preserves the defect. This is an inherited defect in the specifically reviewed/touched path, not a claim that S2a introduced the retry hazard. The blocking verdict follows the requested purity checkpoint; the new remote loop itself otherwise passes the inspected requirements.\n\n**Deterministic reproduction, executed without writing a test file:** construct the minimal dial state consumed by dial-step!, with one append! request and mirror-budget 1. Wrap the real mirror-step to replace the cursor atom's value after its first invocation with an equal cursor carrying different metadata. This changes object identity, forces the swap! CAS to fail, and leaves the logical cursor unchanged. The wrapper performs no source append itself.\n\nObserved:\n```clojure\n{:source-values [:once :once], :answer-count 2, :mirror-invocations 2}\n```\n\nThe following executable reproduction uses only the current implementations plus the stated CAS-conflict instrumentation:\n\n```clojure\n(require '[dao.stream :as s] '[dao.stream.ringbuffer :as rb] '[dao.stream.remote :as remote] '[dao.stream.ws-project :as p])\n(defn ring [] (:dao.stream/handle (rb/create! {:dao.stream/type rb/transport-type rb/capacity-key 16})))\n(defn values [h] (loop [c (:dao.stream/cursor (s/cursor h :dao.stream/oldest)) acc []] (let [r (s/next h c)] (if (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome r)) (recur (:dao.stream/cursor r) (conj acc (:dao.stream/value r))) acc))))\n(let [source (ring) requests (ring) answers (ring) c (:dao.stream/cursor (s/cursor requests :dao.stream/oldest)) cursor-atom (atom c) dial (atom {:ring requests :table {\"w\" {:handle source :surface #{:writer}}} :channel (atom {:handle answers}) :mirror-cursor cursor-atom :mirror-budget 1}) step remote/mirror-step calls (atom 0)]\n(s/append! requests {:dao.stream/identity \"w\" :dao.stream.remote/op :dao.stream/append! :dao.stream.remote/args [:once] :dao.stream.remote/id 1})\n(with-redefs [remote/mirror-step (fn [& args] (let [result (apply step args)] (when (= 1 (swap! calls inc)) (reset! cursor-atom (with-meta @cursor-atom {:force-cas-retry true}))) result))] (p/dial-step! dial))\n(prn {:source-values (values source) :answer-count (count (values answers)) :mirror-invocations @calls}))\n```\n\nA separate two-driver overlap reproduction, synchronizing the first two mirror invocations after their effects and before cursor commits, also produced two source appends and two answer writes for the same request (three mirror invocations, the final retry reading blocked). This demonstrates the caller-overlap hazard in addition to the forced-retry mechanism.\n\n**Requested correction:** compute mirror effects outside atom retry and commit only the resulting cursor through a state-only operation. Follow the explicit driver ownership model; if overlapping driver calls must be supported, serialize the complete tick before executing effects. Merely replacing swap! with reset! does not make concurrent ticks safe. Add a meaningful dial regression demonstrating that a cursor commit conflict cannot re-execute append! or multiply answer writes.\n\n## Checkpoint assessment\n\n| Checkpoint | Assessment and citations |\n|---|---|\n| Budget exhaustion preserves continuation | PASS. Zero remaining returns the current opaque cursor before another read, with no fabricated answer/end (`remote.cljc:298-301`). Successful reads adopt the returned successor cursor (`:314`); gaps adopt the source recovery cursor (`:317`). |\n| Malformed reads and gaps count | PASS. Every ok branch and gap branch recurs with decremented remaining; junk and answer-shaped values consume allowance too (`remote.cljc:301-317`). Blocked/end return without recurring (`:315-316`). |\n| Chase clamp and absent request budget | PASS by inspection and focused tests. Chase requires next, a positive request budget, and an initial ok; its allowance is min(peer, local) (`remote.cljc:236-243`). Without a peer budget it performs no chase. The initial next counts toward k, so clamp 3 yields at most two more outcomes (`:135-151`). |\n| Writer full on idempotent requests | PASS in the remote loop. Returned writer outcome reaches the full check; descriptor, cursor, next and named descriptor stop with the preceding cursor (`remote.cljc:197-204,223-249,305-314`). Oversize replacement's full outcome also propagates (`:161-169`). |\n| Writer full on append! | PASS in the remote loop. append! explicitly bypasses rewind and advances (`remote.cljc:310-314`); the new test proves one source append over sequential ticks (`remote_test.cljc:1762-1777`). Composition retry safety fails R1. |\n| Invalid bounds | PASS. Non-nil non-map bounds and any non-positive/non-integer non-nil map value throw the specified ex-info before channel reads/writes (`remote.cljc:289-295`). Acceptor and dial validate/store their new keys (`ws_project.cljc:211-243,459-488`). |\n| Effects outside atom retry | FAIL at dial-step!, R1. Acceptor mirror execution is outside the state update (`ws_project.cljc:389-401`). |\n| Backward arities/defaults | PASS for budget/chase behavior. Four/five arities delegate with nil (`remote.cljc:285-288`); nil remaining is unbounded and nil chase-max preserves peer allowance. Writer-full rewind is the intentional specified behavior change shared by all arities. |\n| Acceptor/dial wiring | PASS. Acceptor passes both mirror bounds (`ws_project.cljc:391-396`); dial passes step-budget to projection and both mirror bounds (`:582-590`). New integration tests exercise continuation over multiple ticks. |\n| Documentation | Matches implemented S2a signatures, counting, clamp and full handling (`docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:193-257,425-434`). No additional documentation blocker identified. |\n\n## Non-blocking coverage notes\n\n- New full-writer tests directly cover identity descriptor, named descriptor and append!, but not cursor/next (`remote_test.cljc:1734-1777`). Add cursor/next cases, preferably a noninitial rewind position and a bounded chase, to protect the complete stated idempotent set.\n- The chase test covers local clamps 3 and 20 and nil local bounds, but does not directly exercise local clamp 1 or a request without a peer budget under non-nil local bounds (`remote_test.cljc:1706-1731`). The inspected guards are correct.\n- §1.2 also mentions four S2b/S2c link policy keys and a timed dial-step! arity. Their deferral is documented in the implementer's report and depends on later slices; it is not an S2a defect.\n\n## Verification and limits\n\nIndependently ran the focused JVM tests using the existing test classpath, avoiding CLI cache generation:\n```sh\njava -cp \"$(cat .cpcache/3644903311.cp)\" clojure.main \\\n  -m cognitect.test-runner \\\n  -n dao.stream.remote-test -n dao.stream.ws-project-test\n```\n**63 tests, 479 assertions, zero failures, zero errors.**\n\nBoth in-memory adversarial reproductions ran on the JVM and observed duplicate source effects as described above. No reproduction or test source file was created. Full JVM suite, Node/Dart lanes and lint were not independently run in this read-only review. The implementer reports focused JVM success and lint success with one preexisting warning, and explicitly leaves full three-host verification outstanding; this review does not certify the D5 all-host landing gate.\n\n## Disposition\n\nResolve R1 in the dial path and rerun focused tests with a regression for effect execution outside retry. The S2a remote budget/rewind implementation and its wiring otherwise conform to the inspected specification. Cross-host landing verification remains required.\n# Independent S2a review — Round 2\n\n**Verdict: ACCEPT**\n\nReviewed `git diff` in the dedicated worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2`, branch `stream-crossmachine-s2`, HEAD `65f635d0`, including the Round 1 findings and the architecture/build guidance. No code or test files were edited.\n\n## R1 resolved\n\n`src/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc:589` now evaluates `remote/mirror-step` once using `@mirror-cursor`, then stores its returned cursor with `reset!`. The stream reads, source append and answer writes are outside an atom retry function. A cursor atom change during those effects cannot cause their execution to repeat. This follows the existing single-driver ownership model and the accepting composition's approach.\n\nThe new `dial-mirror-effects-run-once-per-step` regression (`test/dao/stream/ws_project_test.cljc:721`) is meaningful: its served writer replaces the cursor with an equal but distinct map during the source append, forcing the old `swap!` implementation's compare-and-set to retry on the JVM. It asserts both one source append and one answer. Independently restoring the old effectful `swap!` behavior through an in-memory `with-redefs` made both assertions fail, observing `[:v :v]` and answer IDs `[1 1]`. The current implementation passes.\n\nThe fix does not serialize overlapping driver calls; acceptance relies on the stated single-driver ownership contract. This was the alternative explicitly allowed by R1, rather than a requirement to support concurrent ticks.\n\n## Coverage additions\n\nThe cursor and next full-writer cases in `test/dao/stream/remote_test.cljc` are sound and idiomatic. Each accepts a preceding descriptor answer, refuses the target answer once, checks that the returned cursor precedes that target rather than returning to the initial cursor, then verifies that the next call answers the same ID without duplicating the earlier answer. The table declares a reader surface and both request argument vectors are valid.\n\nThe optional bounded-chase/full combination, clamp-one case and absent-peer-budget case from Round 1 remain additional coverage opportunities, not blockers. No new correctness finding arose from the updated diff.\n\n## Verification\n\nIndependently ran the requested command:\n\n```sh\nclojure -M:test -n dao.stream.remote-test -n dao.stream.ws-project-test\n```\n\n**64 tests, 487 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.**\n\nThe mutation check above was performed in memory without changing source or test files. An accidental unfiltered runner invocation failed while loading the unavailable generated Python3Lexer; this was not the requested focused run and does not change its successful result. Two initial mutation-check launches had classpath setup errors before the successful check.\n\nFull-suite, Node, Dart and lint verification were not performed in this focused review. Acceptance resolves the Round 1 review findings; it does not certify the separate all-host landing gate.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
